ntly both assigned same side (say side s),\n+        # then moving vertex to other side splits the edge => adds w to cut weight.\n+        # So difference in cut contribution for moving vertex from side S_cur to other side:\n+        # Gain if moved to side 0 = sum_{neighbors assigned 1} w (since edge becomes cut)\n+        # Gain if moved to side 1 = sum_{neighbors assigned 0} w\n+        # In our code above, I defined gain0 as sum over neighbors with assign==0 -> when moving vertex to 0,\n+        # those edges become part of cut, so they contribute +wt. That's correct for gaining by assigning 0.\n+        # Conversely, moving to 1 gives +wt for neighbors assigned 0? Actually need careful.\n+        # Let's recompute clearly:\n+        # Suppose assign[neighbor] = 0 means neighbor on side 0.\n+        # Edge (vertex, neighbor): if both on same side (both 0 or both 1), not in cut.\n+        # If we switch vertex to side 0 while neighbor is also 0, no effect. If we set vertex to 0 while neighbor is 1, edge crosses -> contributes w.\n+        # So gain from setting vertex to side X is sum over neighbors on opposite side Y != X of weight.\n+        # Our current loops:\n+        #   if assign[nb]==0: gain0 += wt   (means neighbor is on side 0)\n+        #       -> setting vertex to side0 would NOT cross? Actually if neighbor is 0 and vertex becomes 0, no crossing.\n+        # So my logic is wrong.\n+        # Let's define:\n+        #   side0 assignment means 0.\n+        #   When evaluating gain for putting vertex on side 0: \n+        #       For each neighbor nb with assign[nb]==1 (neighbor on side1), switching vertex to 0 creates a crossing edge => gain += wt.\n+        #   Similarly for side1: gain += wt for neighbors with assign[nb]==0.\n+        #\n+        # So simply: \n+        #   gain0 = sum_{nb where assign[nb]==1} weight(nb-vertex)\n+        #   gain1 = sum_{nb where assign[nb]==0} weight(nb-vertex)\n+        #\n+        # Implementation: iterate neighbors, check assign of neighbor.\n+        pass  # Will rewrite later\n+\n+    # --- Rewrite greedy section cleanly ---\n+    # Reset adjacency with proper structure for fast iteration\n+    adj_list = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n+    for u, v, w in edges:\n+        adj_list[u].append((v, w))\n+        adj_list[v].append((u, w))\n+\n+    # Compute degrees for biased ordering\n+    degrees = [len(lst) for lst in adj_list]\n+    # Pick nodes with highest degree early, break ties by lower index\n+    order = sorted(range(n), key=lambda i: (-degrees[i], i))\n+\n+    for vertex in order:\n+        # Compute contributions to cut if vertex assigned to side 0 vs 1, given current neighbor assignments.\n+        gain_to_side0 = 0\n+        gain_to_side1 = 0\n+        for nb, wt in adj_list[vertex]:\n+            if assign[nb] == 0:\n+                # neighbor on side0: if we put vertex on side0, edge stays together (no cut); if we put on side1, we get cut w.\n+                # So moving vertex to side1 yields a gain of wt.\n+                gain_to_side1 += wt\n+            else:\n+                # neighbor on side1: moving vertex to side0 yields gain wt.\n+                gain_to_side0 += wt\n+        if gain_to_side0 > gain_to_side1:\n+            assign[vertex] = 0\n+        elif gain_to_side1 > gain_to_side0:\n+            assign[vertex] = 1\n+        else:\n+            assign[vertex] = 0  # tie-break arbitrarily choose 0\n+\n+    return assign\n+\n+\n # OMEGA-BLOCK-END\n",
